1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,286
Welcome to the GopherTrunk Field Guide —
the video edition.

2
00:00:03,406 --> 00:00:07,476
This course is the trunked radio pillar:
the concepts that let many users share a

3
00:00:07,476 --> 00:00:11,545
small pool of channels, explained one idea
at a time.

4
00:00:11,894 --> 00:00:12,765
Here is the route.

5
00:00:12,885 --> 00:00:15,387
We start with trunked radio itself — the
architecture, and why statistics make it

6
00:00:15,387 --> 00:00:17,889
work.

7
00:00:18,009 --> 00:00:21,318
Then the control channel, the data stream
that coordinates everything.

8
00:00:21,438 --> 00:00:24,619
Then talkgroups, the virtual channels you
actually listen to.

9
00:00:24,739 --> 00:00:27,850
Then channel grants, the messages that
start every call.

10
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:33,148
And finally FDMA and TDMA, the two ways
calls share the spectrum.

11
00:00:33,498 --> 00:00:36,720
Every segment stands alone — each one
starts from its own definition, so you can

12
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:39,942
jump straight to the idea you need.

13
00:00:40,062 --> 00:00:44,154
And every segment has a full written
article in the Field Guide at gophertrunk

14
00:00:44,154 --> 00:00:48,247
dot org, linked below, with diagrams,
variants, and sources.

15
00:00:48,597 --> 00:00:49,247
That's the map.

16
00:00:49,367 --> 00:00:51,938
Let's start where the whole idea starts:
with the problem of too many groups and

17
00:00:51,938 --> 00:00:54,510
too few frequencies.
